A||N||8||Zorba||*Grand Dam Brake Upgrade Question *||||6||
Z||000000||Zorba||12-05-2000||11:16 PM||||I have a set of new calipers,pads, rotors for a 88 grand am that I intend to use for a "rear brake" upgrade only. I have heard that this is o.k. but I have also heard about this being 'less than desirable'. Anyone care to comment. I can still return these items to Autozone and get my money back and revert to the original fiero calipers with e-brake and all. Thank you.||||reg||6
Z||000001||stimpy||12-06-2000||12:43 AM||||I'm curious as to why you would want to upgrade the rear brakes and not the front. Isn't that a little backwards?  I can tell you that my dad just did the Baretta/Grand Am swap on his 86 GT and was very impressed with the reslts.[plug]  BTW, anyone living in Northern CA who is interested in this swap can contact my dad at his shop (BigO Tires on Prescott Road in Modesto) and he can hook you up. [/plug]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||12-06-2000||06:17 AM||||Stimpy,<P>I've thought about that upgrade but have questions about whether the proportioning between front and back will be affected and if that matters.<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||12-06-2000||08:49 AM||||From what I have read, it is more common to upgrade both ends, or front brakes only.  Front brakes usually do more work than rears, even on a mid- or rear-engine car.  Installing Grand Am / Beretta brakes on the rear is easier than the front, but less needed, and you lose your emergency brake.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||12-06-2000||12:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>I'm curious as to why you would want to upgrade the rear brakes   [/plug]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Eventually I am doing the front brakes as well. I don't care about the e-brake for now.<BR>I only need to do the rear for now and I thought that the grand am set up would be good until I do the fronts as long as this will not adversely affect the braking system and that is my question to the group. Otherwise, I will return the items to autozone and get a set of new calipers (I already have new rotors and pads for the fiero), they are $46.00 /each. The ones I bought before had a clearance problem and did not fit no matter what. The grand am ones were a piece of cake to bolt on. Auto zone stuff is made in china. If I need to swap back to the original fiero stuff, I will see if Checkers has better calipers. But I think theis is Cardeno's brand.<BR>Since autozone's calipers won't fit right, i thought I would go with the grand am's for now. Later on, I will do the grand am fronts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||MikeE||12-06-2000||06:10 PM||||I'm curious, for those of you who have upgraded the rear brakes, what do you do about the loss of the E-brake?<P>Here in Hawaii, we have yearly safety checks and you must have a working E-brake to pass.  I know some people here find shops that will pass them anyway for cash under the table but those shops don't last long and you then must locate another solution fast.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-06-2000||07:11 PM||||Doing the rear upgrade only will totally hose brake ballance. You absolutly must reproportion the system. You might get away with it on dry pavement but you'll have a huge prtential problem on anything even a little slick.<P>There is no option for the E brake except adding a mechanical spot caliper to the rear. This requires a custom made bracket to carry it and the main. A linelock does not count as an Emergency/parking brake.<P>I've done some digging so far I've not found any state where it is legal to remove the hand brake. It maight get thru some inspections but it still is not legal.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||12-06-2000||07:56 PM||||There are a lot of laws which I think are stupid.. but ones like this are put into effect for a good reason.. safety. I'd like to do an upgrade to the brakes, but I'm not going to try until I have a solution that will allow me to keep the e-brake functioning.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000008||Zorba||12-06-2000||11:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>There are a lot of laws which I think are stupid </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>O.K. Removing the grand am rear calipers, rotors and returning them to Auto Zone tomorrow. The calipers did not clear my custom wheels without some trimming or,worse, a 1/4" wheel adapter, which also didn't fit.<BR>I will buy my calipers from Checkers tomorrow and I am checking out the fiero store to see what they have. My fron end has new brake pads and rotors and it's stock.<BR>With a v8 in my car now I thought I might need extra braking power in the rear where most of the weight is.<BR>So much for this grand am stuff.<P>||||reg||1
